1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,340
 In this lesson, you will start with the simplest form of agentic rag, a router.

2
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:11,940
 Given a query, the router will pick one of several query engines to execute a query.

3
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:16,260
 You'll build a simple router over a single document that can handle both question answering

4
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:18,580
 as well as summarization.

5
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:20,740
 Let's dive in.

6
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:25,500
 Given a query, the router will pick one of several query engines to execute a query.

7
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,460
 So this gives you some dynamic query understanding capabilities.

8
00:00:29,460 --> 00:00:33,980
 We'll show you how to build a very simple router over a single document that can handle

9
00:00:33,980 --> 00:00:37,520
 both question answering as well as summarization over that document.

10
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:41,980
 The first three lessons show you how to build agentic capabilities over a single document.

11
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:45,940
 And the last lesson will show you how to build a multi-document agent.

12
00:00:45,940 --> 00:00:47,340
 So let's get started.

13
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:51,320
 The first thing we'll do is to import our OpenAI keep.

14
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:53,660
 And to do this, we'll define a helper function.

15
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:59,140
 Next, we need to import a special module called Nest AsyncIO.

16
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:03,580
 The reason for this is that Jupyter runs an event loop behind the scenes, and a lot of

17
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:05,180
 our modules use Async.

18
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:09,020
 And to make Async play nice with Jupyter notebooks, we need to import this.

19
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:12,620
 The next step in terms of setup is to load in a sample document.

20
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:19,500
 Here we use MetaGPT, a recently accepted oral paper at iClear 2024 on a cool new multi-agent

21
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:20,500
 framework.

22
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:22,980
 You should definitely check out the paper if you're interested.

23
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:26,980
 Or if you're interested in uploading your own documents, please feel free to do so.

24
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:30,620
 Next, we'll import the sentence splitter from Llama Index.

25
00:01:30,620 --> 00:01:35,380
 The next piece here is we are going to use our simple directory reader module in Llama

26
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:40,980
 Index to read in this PDF into a parsed document representation.

27
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:47,460
 In order to split these documents into even-sized chunks, and we'll split on the order of sentences.

28
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:53,180
 So we set the chunk size to 1024, and we call splitter.getNodesFromDocuments to split these

29
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:56,060
 documents into nodes.

30
00:01:56,060 --> 00:02:01,260
 The next step is optional, and this allows us to define an LLM and embedding model.

31
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:05,860
 And you can do this by specifying a global config setting where you specify the LLM and

32
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:08,820
 embedding model that you want to inject as part of the global config.

33
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:14,540
 By default, we use 3.5 turbo and tax embedding Ada002 in this course, but of course this

34
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:21,020
 allows you to have the groundwork to basically inject your own LLMs as well as embeddings.

35
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:27,020
 So we define the settings object and settings.llm equals OpenAI and settings.embedding model

36
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:29,860
 equals OpenAI embedding.

37
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:33,060
 Now we're ready to start building some indexes.

38
00:02:33,060 --> 00:02:36,620
 Here we define two indexes over these nodes.

39
00:02:36,620 --> 00:02:40,100
 This includes both a summary index and a vector index.

40
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:44,820
 A refresher is that we can think of an index as a set of metadata over our data.

41
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:50,240
 You can query an index, and different indexes will have different retrieval behaviors.

42
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:55,020
 A vector index, for instance, indexes nodes via text embeddings and its core abstraction

43
00:02:55,020 --> 00:02:59,860
 in LLM index and a core abstraction for building any sort of RAG system.

44
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:04,100
 Quering a vector index will return the most similar nodes by embedding similarity.

45
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:08,640
 A summary index, on the other hand, is also a very simple index, but querying it will

46
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:11,660
 return all the nodes currently in the index.

47
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:16,320
 So it doesn't necessarily depend on the user query, but will return all the nodes that's

48
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:19,100
 currently in the index.

49
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:23,220
 To set up both the summary and vector index, it's a matter of importing these two simple

50
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:29,460
 modules.

51
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:34,420
 Now let's turn these indexes into query engines and then query tools.

52
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:39,460
 Each query engine represents overall query interface over the data that's stored in

53
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:43,620
 this index and combines retrieval with LLM synthesis.

54
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:47,700
 Each query engine is good for a certain type of question, and this is a great use case

55
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:52,020
 for a router which can route dynamically between these different query engines.

56
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:56,800
 A query tool now is just the query engine with metadata, specifically a description of

57
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:01,460
 what types of questions the tool can answer.

58
00:04:01,460 --> 00:04:06,980
 So here we define the summary query engine equals summary index.asQueryEngine, and then

59
00:04:06,980 --> 00:04:10,760
 also vector query engine equals vector index.

60
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:14,940
 We can see that the query engine is derived from each of the indexes.

61
00:04:14,940 --> 00:04:19,640
 We can see that for the summary query engine, we set useAsyncEqualsTrue to basically enforce

62
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:23,700
 faster query generation by leveraging async capabilities.

63
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:28,620
 Next, a query tool is just the query engine with metadata.

64
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:33,340
 It's specifically a description of what types of questions the tool can answer.

65
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:39,300
 And we'll define a query tool for both the summary and vector query engines.

66
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:43,120
 Through this code snippet right here, you see that the summary tool description is useful

67
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:48,460
 for summarization questions related to MetaGPT, and the vector tool description is useful

68
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:55,280
 for retrieving specific context from the MetaGPT paper.

69
00:04:55,280 --> 00:04:59,460
 Now that we have our query engines and tools, we're ready to define our router.

70
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:04,500
 Llama Index provides several different types of selectors to enable you to build a router,

71
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:07,540
 and each of these selectors has distinct attributes.

72
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:12,860
 The LLM selector is one option, and it involves prompting an LLM to output a JSON that is

73
00:05:12,860 --> 00:05:16,860
 then parsed, and then the corresponding indexes are queried.

74
00:05:16,860 --> 00:05:20,140
 Another option is to use our Pydantic selectors.

75
00:05:20,140 --> 00:05:24,420
 Instead of directly prompting the LLM with text, we actually use the function calling

76
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:30,740
 APIs supported by models like OpenAI to produce Pydantic selection objects rather than parsing

77
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:31,860
 raw JSON.

78
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:36,380
 For each of these types of selectors, we also have the dynamic capabilities to let you select

79
00:05:36,380 --> 00:05:39,700
 one index to route to, or actually multiple.

80
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:46,020
 Let's try an LLM-powered single selector called LLM single selector.

81
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:47,620
 So we import two modules.

82
00:05:47,620 --> 00:05:52,260
 We import a router query engine as well as the LLM single selector.

83
00:05:52,260 --> 00:05:57,060
 You see that the router query engine takes in a selector type as well as a set of query

84
00:05:57,060 --> 00:05:58,800
 engine tools.

85
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:02,860
 The selector type you see is just the LLM single selector, which means that it prompts

86
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:07,340
 the LLM and makes a single selection, and the query engine tools include the summarization

87
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:11,300
 tool as well as the vector tool.

88
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:14,620
 Now let's try testing out some queries.

89
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:22,140
 The first question we'll ask is, what is the summary of the document?

90
00:06:22,140 --> 00:06:26,660
 The verbose output allows us to view the intermediate steps that are being taken.

91
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:31,860
 We see that the output includes selecting query engine zero useful for summarization

92
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:34,580
 questions related to MetaGPT.

93
00:06:34,580 --> 00:06:38,340
 This means that the first option, the summary tool, is actually picked in order to help

94
00:06:38,340 --> 00:06:40,260
 answer this question.

95
00:06:40,260 --> 00:06:43,060
 And as a result, you're able to get back a response.

96
00:06:43,060 --> 00:06:49,700
 The document introduces MetaGPT, a meta-programming framework for LLM-based multi-agent collaboration.

97
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:54,260
 And this gives an overall summary of the paper and is synthesized over all the context in

98
00:06:54,260 --> 00:06:55,540
 the paper.

99
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:58,220
 So the response comes with sources.

100
00:06:58,220 --> 00:07:02,860
 And to inspect the sources, we can take a look at response source nodes.

101
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:06,820
 When we take a look at the length of response source nodes, we see that the length is equal

102
00:07:06,820 --> 00:07:08,180
 to 34.

103
00:07:08,180 --> 00:07:13,300
 Coincidentally, this is exactly equal to the number of chunks of the entire document.

104
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:16,980
 And so we see that the summary query engine must have been getting called because the

105
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:23,300
 summary query engine returns all the chunks corresponding to the items within its index.

106
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:24,980
 Let's take a look at another example.

107
00:07:24,980 --> 00:07:29,420
 We'll ask the question, how do agents share information with other agents?

108
00:07:29,420 --> 00:07:34,820
 So let's ask this against the overall router query engine and take a look at both the verbose

109
00:07:34,820 --> 00:07:37,340
 output as well as the response.

110
00:07:37,340 --> 00:07:41,060
 Here we see that we actually select query engine 1.

111
00:07:41,060 --> 00:07:45,500
 And the LLM gives some reasoning as to why it actually picked the vector search tool

112
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:47,540
 as opposed to the summary tool.

113
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:53,180
 It's because it focuses on retrieving specific context from the MetaGPT paper, where agents

114
00:07:53,180 --> 00:07:57,180
 sharing information with other agents is probably located within a paragraph of that paper.

115
00:07:57,180 --> 00:08:00,260
 And you see that it's able to find that context and generate a response.

116
00:08:00,260 --> 00:08:06,220
 So you're able to utilize a shared message tool where they can publish structured messages.

117
00:08:06,220 --> 00:08:08,920
 And that basically helps to conclude lesson one.

118
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:13,960
 To put everything together, all the code above can be consolidated into a single helper function

119
00:08:13,960 --> 00:08:19,700
 that takes in a file path and builds the router query engine with both vector search and summarization

120
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:21,020
 over it.

